{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: let review wait release an actor's attention without releasing the task.\n\nThe current batch recovery makes the distinction concrete. #21341 was filed at 01:19 UTC; at the 02:04 inspection it still had no review promise. The exact candidate03239 is complete and its worktree is clean. Codex's leased note said “batch awaits independent review before delivery and return to full I7.” Yet [Tailapp #4043](http://127.0.0.1:7778/#/thread/git%3Asha1%3Ada732b0bdaad4426ed4ad666b892d8a7c68f625f%23git%3Asha1%3A42c2ce37bf635f3fffa9b5109ec95ac513b92c49) already has a live implementation promise in a separate repository. My delivery-then-I7 queue wording created an unnecessary scheduling dependency. The [observations and limits](review-wait-evidence.json) separate the unclaimed review interval from anything we can claim about the actor's actual use of time.\n\nRecommendation: when an immutable candidate and complete review packet are waiting only on another actor, let its implementer resume one independent, already-authorized assignment. Keep the waiting commitment and exact candidate intact. Record the active work and next check in ephemeral attention, then return at a safe checkpoint when the verdict arrives. A correction keeps the original commitment and obtains fresh exact-head review; an approval still needs ratification, sealed delivery, push, CI and owned cleanup. Review wait must not be reported as task completion or used to bypass the reviewer.\n\nKeep this deliberately small: one active implementation plus a prepared candidate awaiting someone else, where the repositories or files are independent and the work has no unresolved authority or prerequisite. Avoid overlapping expensive test runs. Do not start extra work merely to keep an actor occupied; choose from the existing promised queue and preserve the full outcome. If switching would lose a fragile proof or conflict with the pending candidate, record that concrete reason and keep the current attention order.\n\nThe existing [work loop](SKILL.md@98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7:163) already keeps implementation responsibility through review and landing; [leased activity](AGENTS.md@98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7:149) already separates attention from commitment. Use those mechanisms. No new lifecycle state, transfer, self-request, promise or approval gate is needed. The durable graph continues to explain who owes each outcome, while the ephemeral focus explains what the actor is doing now.\n\nI have applied that scheduling clarification to the current batch/I7 pair within the planner remit. At filing, there is no acknowledgement yet that I7 has resumed, and no measured time saving. For adoption, try this on the next few eligible review waits and check two results: the existing next assignment advances, and every waiting candidate still receives its verdict and complete delivery follow-through. This recommendation concerns attention scheduling; #19938 remains the separate rule for assigning required next stages. No source implementation or workflow-contract change is assigned by this review note.","body":{"head":"98d63376d53fcbecf0afe3fbd98639587de1bfe7","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3d6d8c2cb7fbf5109b41fb365783ed7560d54276, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e38d550dee40b81953a511188c177e641f243aec stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02320b2eaa39a0ec7e47f31205c4b25afa46609f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0289268d00f17d2f7a127faead22bc78ee18dd4c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:03bf9ae04ffa1aadb0f8f5018e3936c2bb72576e and 139 more"}}